"Application should follow secure coding practice wherever applicable."

We confirm that our development team strictly follows secure coding practices to ensure the robustness and security of our application, complying with industry standards (such as OWASP guidelines) wherever applicable. 

Below are the key measures and secure coding practices we have implemented:

Input Validation & Sanitization: We treat all user input as untrusted. Strict allow-lists, data type validation, and sanitization are applied on the server-side to prevent injection attacks (SQLi, XSS).
Parameterized Queries: To prevent SQL injection, all database interactions use prepared statements (parameterized queries) rather than string concatenation.
Authentication & Session Management: We enforce strong authentication, secure session management with secure cookies (HttpOnly, Secure flags), and implement proper session timeouts.
Data Protection: Sensitive data is encrypted both in transit (using TLS 1.3/HTTPS) and at rest.
Error Handling & Logging: Detailed errors are never displayed to the end-user to prevent information leakage. Instead, we use generic error messages, while logging detailed, sanitized debug information on secure internal servers.


